{
  "widgets": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 24,
      "height": 3,
      "properties": {
        "markdown": "# CI host \u2014 Jenkins EC2 (the shared CI host)\n\nJenkins and five GitHub Actions runners share this machine. **Swap growth is the signal that matters** \u2014 the runners are denied swap by their cgroup, so anything paged out is Jenkins, and Jenkins stays slow until it pages back in. Available memory near zero and swap climbing together mean the box is oversubscribed, not that a build is heavy.\n\n`RunnersDown` above zero is silent damage: a repository whose runner is registered but not listening has its jobs queue for **24 hours** and then die, with no failure to look at.\n\nPublished every 5 minutes by `publish-metrics.sh` on the host. Metrics resolution is 5 minutes, so a short spike inside a build can be missed.\n\n**Load high while CPU is idle means blocked on disk, not short of cores.** That is how the root volume was found to be magnetic at ~100 IOPS with a queue of 3.3 \u2014 three concurrent `npm ci` runs waiting on it while the containers sat at 3% CPU. `VolumeQueueLength` comes free from AWS; it needs no publisher."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "metric",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "width": 6,
      "height": 4,
      "properties": {
        "title": "Jenkins alive",
        "view": "singleValue",
        "region": "us-east-1",
        "stat": "Minimum",
        "period": 300,
        "sparkline": true,
        "metrics": [
          [
            "Answering/CIHost",
            "JenkinsUp",
            "InstanceId",
            "INSTANCE_ID_PLACEHOLDER",
            {
              "label": "1 = running"
            }
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
